## How a round works

1. Write the plan first, then work through it. No stop for approval
2. Branch off main, one branch per round or per fix. Nothing is committed or pushed on main
3. Run the full tests after the last edit. Keep the counts before and after
4. breaker over the changed files, reviewer over the change, claim-checker over the log entry
5. A new entry at the top of steps/log.md, and steps/PROGRESS.md rewritten, never appended
6. One pull request. Merge it yourself once the gate is green, passing the squash title and message.
   Never merge red, never leave one open, never open a second while one is open
7. Where the host side changed, steps/03_your_steps.md is rewritten for the run on the machine

## Rules for every file

- Core holds no host type, and every rule in it has a test. A change to the host project is read twice
  and proved by a run on the machine
- Every number that shapes a run is a setting, never a constant
- Nothing in the code names one project's file
- The tool reports what it noticed and changes nothing until [Name] presses the button
- One rule lives in one place. A copy of a rule in a second file is a bug
- A file the tool did not write is never listed as written, and a size is logged only after it is read
  back off the disk
- [evidence folders] are never edited. A hook refuses it

